2026-07-25 -- Estimate grid: event delegation, non-destructive saves, message log
================================================================================

Headline: editing the Parts & Labor grid no longer fights you.

The focus bug (top of last run's list):
  * Every field on the grid autosaves on change; the save round-trips the whole
    estimate and renderEstimate() redrew the entire grid from the response.
    That meant tabbing Description -> Qty fired the Description save, and a few
    hundred ms later its response blew away the DOM you were now standing in --
    caret gone, and whatever you'd typed into Qty in the meantime with it.
  * renderEstimate() now records a *shape* signature: item ids, kinds, job
    assignment, status, returned flag, received-invoice, and whether the
    receive checkbox should exist. Deliberately NOT in it: description, part #,
    qty, cost, core -- the values that live inside the inputs being edited.
  * applyEstimateResponse() is the single "the server gave us an estimate"
    entry point. Same shape (the overwhelmingly common case) ->
    syncEstimateInPlace() writes the server's values into the existing controls,
    skipping document.activeElement, and recomputes job subtotals + the
    Quoted/Actual lines. Nothing is destroyed, no flicker, focus untouched.
  * Shape actually changed (line added/deleted, status flipped, job renamed,
    rows reordered) -> full render, but wrapped in captureEstimateFocus() /
    restoreEstimateFocus(), which re-finds the field by row id (falling back to
    row position for a not-yet-saved row) and restores the caret offsets.

Event delegation:
  * renderEstimate() used to bind ~12 listeners per row plus 3 per job on every
    render -- which is also why the redraw was so expensive. All of it now
    lives in wireEstimateGrid(), one delegated change/click/drag set on
    #vd-estimate-items, wired once at startup. The container is never replaced,
    only its contents, so every row is live the instant it's painted (including
    addEstimateRow's transient row, which had to hand-wire its own × button).
  * wireJobControls() is gone; its three handlers became onJobTechnicianChange
    / onJobEdit / onJobDelete, reading the job from state.detail.order rather
    than from a closure captured at render time.
  * Drag-and-drop is delegated too. The old code wired each .kind-subgroup
    separately so a row could only be dropped among its own group's siblings;
    the delegated version gets the same guarantee from a
    row.parentNode !== dragRow.parentNode check.
  * .ei-status carries data-prev so the delegated handler can put the select
    back when the PATCH fails (it used to close over the pre-change value).

Autosave indicator:
  * #vd-estimate-save-state next to the "Parts & Labor" heading: "Saving…"
    while a request is in flight, "All changes saved" that fades after 2.2s,
    and a red "Not saved" that stays. Autosave being invisible also meant
    there was no way to tell a successful save from the app ignoring you.

Message log (topbar bell):
  * Toasts died after 3.2s with no history -- an error that fired while the
    advisor was looking at the keyboard was gone for good. Every toast now also
    appends to a 50-entry log behind a bell in the topbar, with timestamps and
    errors marked. The unread badge counts errors only; a wall of successful
    saves shouldn't be what makes you go look. Opening the panel marks read.
  * The topbar's right-hand controls are now actually right-aligned
    (margin-left:auto on .notif) instead of floating mid-bar; dropped the
    .topbar-right / .who-badge rules, which nothing had used in a while.

Details drawer:
  * Preference is remembered per width bucket now (dao-details-open /
    dao-details-open-narrow, threshold 1240px, wide defaults open and narrow
    defaults closed). A window that was maximised when you opened the drawer
    no longer drags that choice down to a half-width window, where the drawer
    squeezes the estimate grid into its stacked card layout. Crossing the
    threshold re-applies that bucket's choice. matchMedia is feature-detected.

Tests: 167 -> 169, and the DOM smoke test roughly doubled.
  * test_static_assets.py: no .addEventListener may reappear inside
    renderEstimate (that's the regression that reintroduces the focus loss),
    and wireEstimateGrid() must exist and be called from init. Both
    mutation-tested.
  * tests/dom/estimate_smoke.mjs now drives the real autosave loop against a
    scriptable fetch: it asserts estimateShape ignores editable values but
    notices status/return/received changes; that a same-shape save keeps focus,
    keeps the same DOM nodes, keeps the focused field's text, and still pushes
    the server's value into an *unfocused* field; that a shape change redraws
    and restores focus anyway; that the delegated × button works on rows that
    never had a listener bound; and the message log's ordering, unread count,
    open-to-read, click-outside and HTML escaping. All mutation-tested (killing
    the in-place path, the focus restore, or the delegated click each fail it).
  * Harness bug found and fixed: the smoke test dispatched its own
    DOMContentLoaded, but jsdom fires the real one a tick after the constructor
    returns -- so the app's entire init ran TWICE and every startup listener was
    doubled. That's what made the new bell appear dead (two toggle handlers,
    second one undoing the first). It now waits for jsdom's own event.

Suite green with and without jsdom; server boots clean and /, /assets/*,
/api/health, /api/dashboard, /api/vehicles-board all verified over real HTTP.

Next time, in rough priority order:
  1. Now that a save doesn't redraw, the change-event granularity is worth
     revisiting: a debounced input listener on the money/qty fields would make
     totals update as you type instead of only on blur.
  2. Extend the jsdom harness to the vehicles board and A/P screens -- adding
     a screen is ~20 lines now that the fetch mock is scriptable.
  3. The message log is session-only. Persisting it (localStorage, capped)
     would make "what was that error before I refreshed?" answerable, and gives
     somewhere to put a Copy button for support.
  4. The same shape/sync trick would help the vehicles table, which re-renders
     wholesale on every filter chip and loses scroll position doing it.
  5. Keyboard: Tab out of the last row's last field should add a new line
     (every shop-management product does this); right now you have to reach
     for ＋ Part with the mouse.


2026-07-25 — Vehicles board rebuilt as a real work list
-------------------------------------------------------
The Parts & Labor grid has had three runs of attention now; the board you
land on had had none. It was a table with one sortable column, two listeners
bound per row on every keystroke, and no way to drive it from the keyboard.
This run rebuilt it.

Sorting:
  * All eight columns sort, not just Age. One comparator per column in
    VEHICLE_SORTS (text vs number), and the click cycle is desc -> asc ->
    off, so a third click puts you back on the server's newest-first order
    instead of trapping you in a sort you didn't want.
  * Text columns compare case-insensitively with numeric collation ("R-99"
    before "R-100") and always sort blanks last in *both* directions. A
    we-owe with no stock number is missing data, not the alphabetically
    first car, and floating it to the top on an ascending sort is what the
    old single-column sort would have done.
  * aria-sort on the active header, a placeholder-width arrow so headers
    don't shift sideways when you sort them, and a title that tells you what
    the next click does.

Incremental render:
  * renderVehiclesTable() used to rebuild tbody.innerHTML on every keystroke,
    chip and sort click, then re-bind two listeners per row. Now each <tr> is
    keyed by "segment:id" and carries data-sig, a signature of every field
    the row template reads. Unchanged rows are *moved* rather than rebuilt,
    changed rows get their cells replaced, only genuinely new rows are
    created, and the scroll offset is captured and restored around the whole
    thing. Sorting a 60-car board no longer throws you back to the top of it.
  * All row events are one delegated click handler on #vehicles-table, since
    a recycled row would otherwise either double up listeners or lose them
    depending on which side of the reuse it landed on.

Selection:
  * Shift+click extends from the last row touched, in *displayed* order
    (via visibleVehicles(), so it follows the current sort rather than the
    server's). Ctrl/Cmd+click toggles a row without navigating.
  * Select-all takes what the filter is showing, not every row in memory --
    it used to walk the DOM checkboxes, which was accidentally right, and
    would have gone quietly wrong the moment the table virtualized.
  * The header checkbox goes indeterminate on a partial selection.

Keyboard: ↑/↓ (and j/k) move a cursor row, Home/End jump, Enter opens, Space
  selects, Esc clears the selection, "/" focuses search and Esc inside search
  clears it. The cursor is styled distinctly from hover *and* from selection,
  since all three can land on the same row. A legend sits under the table
  (hidden on touch widths, where none of it applies).

Also: a Status filter built from the statuses actually present in the data
  (a hardcoded list would offer dead options — recon and we-owe don't share
  a status vocabulary); a Quoted column beside Cost, with Cost turning red
  when a car has run >10% past its estimate, which is the number the board
  is actually read for; segment/status/sort persisted to localStorage and
  restored *before* the first fetch, so a saved History filter loads the
  archived side rather than loading the live board and then discovering it;
  a "Reset view" button that appears only when something is filtered; sticky
  table header in a scroll container; Quoted hidden below 1000px.

Tests: 169 -> 176.
  * tests/dom/harness.mjs — the jsdom boot (including the DOMContentLoaded
    subtlety) extracted out of estimate_smoke.mjs, plus press()/click()
    helpers and an unhandledRejection recorder, so a render that throws
    inside a click handler is reported instead of killing the process
    mid-suite. test_dom_smoke.py now discovers *_smoke.mjs by glob and runs
    one test case per screen; adding a screen is a new file and nothing else.
  * tests/dom/board_smoke.mjs — 50-odd assertions over sorting (including
    blanks-last and numeric-vs-lexicographic), node reuse, over-quote
    flagging, segment+status composition, select-all vs. filter, preference
    round-trip, click/shift/ctrl selection, the whole keyboard map, both
    empty states, and HTML escaping of vehicle fields. Mutation-tested:
    killing node reuse, flipping blanks-first, and making select-all ignore
    the filter each fail it.
  * test_static_assets.py: the header's column count is now pinned to
    vehicleRowHtml's cells, to the empty state's colspan, and to the loading
    skeleton's width; every data-sort-key must have a VEHICLE_SORTS entry;
    and renderVehiclesTable may not contain addEventListener. All bite.

Suite green (176), server boots, and the board was rendered in jsdom against
a real seeded /api/vehicles-board response to confirm it paints.

Next time, in rough priority order:
  1. The stat cards still summarize the whole board while the table below
     them is filtered. Either scope them to the current filter or label them
     as lot-wide -- right now they silently disagree with the list.
  2. Rows are cheap to render but there are still no columns for what the
     board is missing: days since last activity (updated_at is already in
     the payload) and an at-a-glance parts-waiting indicator.
  3. Now that harness.mjs exists, the A/P and Cores/Returns screens are ~20
     lines of fixture away from having the same coverage.
  4. Debounced input on the estimate's money/qty fields so totals update as
     you type rather than on blur (carried over -- still worth doing).
  5. Tab out of the last estimate row should add a new line; you still have
     to reach for ＋ Part with the mouse.
  6. The message log is session-only; persisting it (localStorage, capped)
     would make "what was that error before I refreshed?" answerable.

