2026-07-25 -- The vehicles board tells the truth, and says what it's waiting on
================================================================================

Two things this run. The first was the item at the top of last run's list; the
second is the answer to the question the board couldn't answer.

1. The summary cards now describe the rows on screen.

   They were computed from every in-progress vehicle in state.vehicles while
   the table below them was filtered by segment, status and search. Filter to
   We-Owe and the card still read "14 in recon"; search for one car and Cost
   still showed the lot's total. Reports has described its own rows since it
   was rebuilt, so the two screens gave different answers for the same
   question, which makes both of them untrustworthy rather than one of them
   wrong. renderStats() takes the visible rows now and is called from inside
   renderVehiclesTable(), so it can't go stale on a filter click -- called
   once at fetch time, which is where it used to live, it would.

   The four cards changed with them, because "In Recon / Open We-Owe" as two
   separate cards stops meaning anything once the board is filtered to one
   segment:

     Vehicles          count, split "3 recon · 2 we-owe"
     Waiting on Parts  vehicles with a part on order, $ on order  (warn)
     Cost              actual, sub "of $X quoted"  -- the comparison inline
     Over Quote        cars past estimate, $ past  (crit)

   Colour is driven by the number rather than by position, so a zero card is
   quiet and an orange one always means go look at something. Underneath them
   a scope line spells out what's included ("Showing vehicles: recon ·
   waiting on parts.") so "2 vehicles" can't be read as the size of the lot.
   It describes the filters, not the result, so it still explains an empty
   board -- and deliberately doesn't agree its noun with the count, which
   would produce "Showing vehicle: we-owe" whenever a filter left one car.

   Over Quote counts exactly the red Cost cells: isOverQuote() is now the one
   definition of the >10%-past-estimate rule. Reports had quietly grown a
   second copy whose comment claimed it was the same rule as the board's --
   it differed in whether it tested actual_cost, agreed with the board only
   by accident, and now goes through the same predicate.

2. "Waiting on parts" -- a new column, a filter, and a card.

   The single most common reason a car sits on the lot, and until now the
   only way to find out was to open the ticket and read the estimate.
   cost_rollup() returns parts_pending / parts_pending_value: part lines with
   status 'ordered', which is precisely "a vendor owes the shop this". Quoted
   lines nobody has ordered aren't pending, labor is never pending, received
   has landed, and returned lines drop out for the same reason they drop out
   of cost. Voided ROs don't count, same as cost.

   On screen: a count badge in a new Parts column (blank, not a dash, on the
   rows that aren't waiting on anything -- on a full board most aren't, and
   50 dashes would draw the eye to exactly the wrong rows), the dollar value
   on the tooltip, a sortable column, and a "Waiting on parts" toggle chip
   beside the segment chips. The toggle composes with them rather than
   replacing them, and has its own empty state: with it on, "No recon
   vehicles" would be a lie about the lot when what's true is that none of
   them are waiting on a part -- which is good news and now reads as such.

   That toggle is a .chip sitting in the same row as the four mutually
   exclusive segment chips, which is the shape that goes wrong: the
   radio-group wiring and the prefs loader both collected chips by class, so
   an unscoped selector would clear it on every segment click and light it up
   on every page load. All three selectors ask for [data-filter] now, and
   there's a test that fails if any of them stops.

Things found on the way:

  * BOARD_COLUMNS. The loading skeleton's column count and the empty state's
    colspan were separate literals; adding the Parts column made both wrong,
    and the existing tests caught it, which is what they're for. One constant
    now, with the test rewritten to hold *it* to the <thead> instead of
    checking two hardcoded numbers that can drift apart from each other.

  * A responsive rule hid board column nth-child(8) -- Quoted. Inserting
    Parts ahead of it silently moved that rule onto Cost. Targeted by class
    now, so it survives the next column anyone adds.

  * The seed data never had a lot vehicle waiting on parts: the only two
    'ordered' part lines were on a retail ticket, which has no recon or
    we-owe vehicle and so never reaches the board. The feature was invisible
    in the demo. R-1002 (mid-repair, "needs brakes and a windshield") now has
    its parts on order, and the running-boards we-owe got the only live
    ticket on that half of the board, so both segments show the column.

  * tests/dom/harness.mjs collected failures until finish() printed them, so
    a smoke test that died partway -- typically querySelector().click() on an
    element the broken code never rendered -- exited with nothing but a
    TypeError stack, hiding the half-dozen assertions that had already
    recorded exactly what was wrong. It prints what it has on an uncaught
    exception now. Two of this run's mutation tests were unreadable before
    this and legible after.

Tests: 183 -> 192, and everything new was mutation-tested.
  * test_recon.py -- three cases walking a part through quoted -> ordered ->
    received -> returned, plus voided ROs and the we-owe half of the board.
    Mutations killed: counting quoted lines, counting labor, counting
    returned lines, counting voided ROs. The labor mutation survived the
    first draft (labor never reaches 'ordered' by the order-parts route) so
    the test now sets it through the per-line status endpoint, which doesn't
    look at kind.
  * board_smoke.mjs -- fixture extended so every card is checkable by hand
    (5 vehicles, $2,940 of $3,000, 2 waiting worth $425, 1 over by $550; the
    Camry at $310 against $300 is the inside-the-10%-band case). Asserts the
    cards follow segment/status/search, the Over Quote card equals the count
    of red cells, badge rendering and pluralisation, the parts sort, and the
    toggle's filtering, composition, persistence, restore and empty state.
    Mutations killed: cards fed the whole board, the 10% band dropped, the
    parts filter no-op'd, the toggle swept into the segment chips, the toggle
    not persisted, the toggle not restored.
  * test_static_assets.py -- five new checks pinning renderStats to the
    visible rows, the card ids to index.html, the single over-quote rule,
    [data-filter] on every segment selector, and the prefs round-trip. All
    seven mutations caught.

Suite green (192; the 3 jsdom cases run via node), server boots, and the
board was rendered in jsdom against a real seeded database -- 8 rows, both
badges on the right cars, toggle and cards behaving.

Sandbox notes for next run: `uv` can't reach the Python build host here, but
`pip install --break-system-packages fastapi uvicorn httpx pytest` works and
the app runs fine on 3.10 despite requires-python >=3.12. jsdom: install to
/tmp and point NODE_PATH at it; the repo's tests/dom/node_modules hangs on
read over the mount. Background processes don't survive between shell calls,
so start the server and drive it in the same call, and the full suite needs
splitting across two calls to fit the timeout.

