================================================================================
2026-07-25 -- Idle time: a real last-activity clock, and the board's first chart
================================================================================

Started by committing last hour's work, which was finished but uncommitted (a
stale .git/index.lock -- see the sandbox notes -- had blocked the commit). Ran
the suite against it first: green at 192, committed as 145f228, then started
this run's work on top.

Took carried items 1 and 2 together, because item 1 (the board has no chart)
had no honest thing to chart until item 2 (aging is measured from acquisition,
so it says nothing about whether anyone is working on the car) was fixed.

Backend -- orders.last_activity_at.
  * New column, migrated in place and backfilled from coalesce(max(activity
    event), created_at). The backfill is scoped to rows still '' so it re-runs
    harmlessly forever, which also covers a restore from a pre-column backup.
  * workflow.touch_order() bumps it; record_activity() calls touch_order, so
    every route that writes to the activity log gets it for free (status,
    notes, assignment, inspection, findings, void, authorization, invoice,
    payment, jobs, all of parts.py). save_estimate calls it directly -- writing
    the grid is the most common thing anyone does to a ticket and the one
    mutation that deliberately logs nothing, because it autosaves and would
    bury the log.
  * recon.last_activity() = newest last_activity_at across the vehicle's
    non-voided tickets. Voided is excluded for the same reason cost_rollup
    excludes it: voiding means the work never happened, and voiding is itself
    an activity event, so a cancelled ticket would otherwise be the newest
    thing on the car.
  * Board rows carry last_activity_at + idle_days.

The fallback for a car with no ticket was the interesting bug, and I only found
it by pointing the thing at real seeded data. First cut used
`updated_at or created_at`. updated_at moves on any record patch, and for a car
with no ticket it's the *only* timestamp there is -- so all four seeded we-owe
promises and two recon cars, none of which have a ticket, reported idle=0. The
worst cars on the lot were the ones the column hid. It's created_at now: a car
nobody has written a ticket for is idle since it landed, and correcting its VIN
doesn't reset that.

Front end -- Idle column + the activity chart.
  * Idle column beside Age, sortable, "today" rather than "0d", tooltip naming
    the date. Tighter thresholds than Age (warn at 3 days, past a weekend;
    crit at 7) because two weeks of nothing on a recon car is a crisis, not a
    warning. idleClass() derives its colors from IDLE_BUCKETS instead of
    carrying its own numbers, so a row's color and the bar it lands in can't
    drift -- same discipline as isOverQuote and the Over Quote card.
  * The board's first chart: five idle buckets, collapsible, persisted. Every
    bar is a filter (click or Enter/Space, click again to clear). barChart()
    grew optional rowAttrs/rowClass hooks so Reports is untouched.
  * The two traps I aimed at deliberately: the chart counts over
    visibleVehicles({ignoreIdle:true}), so picking a bucket narrows the table
    without collapsing the chart that picked it (and empty buckets stay,
    greyed, so it doesn't reflow under your cursor); and hiding the chart
    clears the bucket, because that filter can only be seen or cleared from
    the chart and would otherwise leave rows missing with no visible cause.
  * Bars stopPropagation on Enter/Space -- the board binds both on document
    (open the cursor row / select it), so activating a filter would also open
    a car. Related: picking a bucket no longer nulls the cursor, so a cursor
    that survives the filter keeps its place.
  * Age hides under 900px, Idle stays: both are day counts, but idle is the
    one you act on. The table's scroll container gives up height while the
    chart is open, or the page grows a second scrollbar and the sticky header
    becomes unreachable.

seed_mock_data.py now ages the board. Every record is created through the API,
so everything was timestamped "now" -- every Age read 0d and the whole time
dimension of the board demoed as one flat bar. A final pass backdates the eight
live vehicles (6-41 days) and the three that have tickets (0-4 days idle), which
puts at least one car in every bucket. Idle is only stated for cars with a
ticket; for the rest it correctly follows age, which is what caught the
fallback bug above.

Tests: 192 -> 201, everything new mutation-tested (17 mutations, 17 killed
after two rounds -- notes below on the two that first survived).
  * test_recon.py -- seven cases: idle from ticket activity and reset by
    working it, a car with no ticket idle since acquisition, patching the
    vehicle record not resetting it, estimate saves counting, voided tickets
    excluded, we-owe rows carrying it, garbage timestamps not 500ing the board.
    conftest grew a db_path fixture so a test can backdate a timestamp instead
    of faking the clock.
  * test_db_migration.py -- backfill picks the newest event (not the first or
    last inserted), falls back to created_at, and never overwrites a live value.
  * board_smoke.mjs -- fixture's idle values are deliberately in a different
    order from its ages (a column quietly reading age_days passes a
    same-order fixture and fails this one), and one car per bucket so every
    bar is a 1. ~40 new assertions over the column, the tooltip, severity,
    sorting, the bars, bucket filtering, composition with the segment chips,
    persistence, the empty state and the collapse-clears-filter rule.
  * test_static_assets.py -- five checks pinning the column end to end, the
    single set of thresholds, the bars' filter wiring, collapse-clears-filter,
    and the prefs round-trip.

Two mutations survived their first attempt and are worth remembering, because
both were the test being wrong rather than the code:
  * Removing save_estimate's touch_order survived -- I'd run the file with
    `-k idle` and the test is named ..._counts_as_activity. Run the whole file.
  * Removing the bars' stopPropagation survived because pickBucket used to null
    the cursor, so the document handler had nothing to act on -- the guard was
    real but unobservable. Fixed by not nulling the cursor (better behaviour
    anyway) and parking it on a row that survives the filter the bar applies.

Suite green (201; the 3 jsdom cases run via node), server boots, and the board
was rendered in jsdom against the real seeded database -- 8 rows, all five bars
scaling correctly, tones on the right rows, and clicking 14+ narrowing to 3
rows with the chart intact.

Sandbox notes for next run (updated):
  * Deletes fail on the mount ("Operation not permitted"), which breaks git:
    every ref update leaves HEAD.lock/index.lock behind and the *next* command
    then refuses to run. Renames work, so sweep the locks aside instead of
    deleting them. There's a wrapper at ~/g.sh that does this around any git
    command; without it you'll hit "Unable to create '.git/index.lock'".
    Same reason `git checkout -- <file>` can't restore a file: mutation
    testing has to keep its own copy (~/pymutate.sh).
  * /tmp persists between runs but the previous run's files are owned by
    `nobody` and unwritable -- don't reuse a path a previous run created
    (/tmp/proj, /tmp/domrun.sh). ~/ is writable.
  * jsdom: `npm install jsdom` into /tmp, then run the smokes from a scratch
    tree with NODE_PATH set. Running them in place hangs -- node resolves
    tests/dom/node_modules first and reads on it never return over the mount,
    which also hangs `pytest tests/test_dom_smoke.py` and even `ls -la` in
    that directory. ~/domrun.sh handles the copy.
  * pip install --break-system-packages fastapi uvicorn httpx pytest; uv can't
    reach its Python build host. Bash calls cap at 45s, so the suite needs
    splitting across two calls and background servers don't survive between
    calls (start and curl in the same call).

